00:14:41.428 --> 00:14:44.688
So those are all subtle things in
the transcript that we're looking for, but

00:14:44.688 --> 00:14:47.214
I would also take take us out
of the transcript for a minute.

00:14:47.214 --> 00:14:50.898
One of the other places that
intellectual curiosity will surface for

00:14:50.898 --> 00:14:54.088
us is actually through your
extracurricular activities.

00:14:54.088 --> 00:14:56.776
If you have hobbies or
different organizations that

00:14:56.776 --> 00:15:00.510
you might be involved with outside of
work and outside of the classroom.

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:04.890
That can actually pop there too because
oftentimes you're drawn to different

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:08.748
activities because you have
a particular interest or passion in it.

00:15:08.748 --> 00:15:10.974
So it does surface in many places.

00:15:10.974 --> 00:15:15.736
&gt;&gt; And you can also say that for work,
too, like what are some insights you might

00:15:15.736 --> 00:15:19.994
have had that have shaped the way
a project or outcome has turned out?

00:15:19.994 --> 00:15:24.426
It's this broad concept of ideas, maybe?

00:15:24.426 --> 00:15:26.707
And what are the ideas that excite you,

00:15:26.707 --> 00:15:30.650
knowing that some ideas are going to
be explored in the classroom, and

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:35.237
some might actually be more exciting and
dynamic in that professional realm?

00:15:35.237 --> 00:15:37.003
So really thinking broadly about that.

00:15:37.003 --> 00:15:41.736
So I love that and we actually already
started to go into transcripts.

00:15:41.736 --> 00:15:44.861
And as someone that I highly respect and

00:15:44.861 --> 00:15:49.039
invites conversation like this,
let's go there.

00:15:49.039 --> 00:15:52.146
[LAUGH] So
let's talk about the transcripts.

00:15:52.146 --> 00:15:55.255
Allison, you started,
Talking about GPA and

00:15:55.255 --> 00:15:57.778
looking beyond just that single number.

00:15:57.778 --> 00:15:59.928
GPA is a single data point, but

00:15:59.928 --> 00:16:04.574
actually doesn't tell me much
when I'm reading an application.

00:16:04.574 --> 00:16:08.868
The transcript itself as a whole is so
much more interesting and has so

00:16:08.868 --> 00:16:11.836
much more data and
information about you to it.

00:16:11.836 --> 00:16:16.229
Can we talk a little bit about how
we look at kind of the coursework,

00:16:16.229 --> 00:16:20.088
trends anything like that
within the transcript itself?

00:16:20.088 --> 00:16:25.664
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, we talked some about
taking classes outside your major.

00:16:25.664 --> 00:16:29.949
I think we do look at
the rigor of your major and so

00:16:29.949 --> 00:16:35.535
you may have been pre-med or
electrical engineering major.

00:16:35.535 --> 00:16:38.571
So the GPA may be lower, but

00:16:38.571 --> 00:16:44.117
I guess what I'm saying is
we look at the context.

00:16:44.117 --> 00:16:46.738
We look at your performance within
the context, so maybe tough major.

00:16:46.738 --> 00:16:52.865
So maybe you were the first person in
your family to ever go to college.

00:16:52.865 --> 00:16:57.202
Maybe your family
immigrated to the States so

00:16:57.202 --> 00:17:00.859
English isn't your first language.

00:17:00.859 --> 00:17:05.681
So we really look at the broader
context in terms of things that may

00:17:05.681 --> 00:17:09.378
have contributed or
affected your performance.

00:17:09.378 --> 00:17:12.729
&gt;&gt; I'm actually going to expand that
context even further because I know

00:17:12.729 --> 00:17:15.303
there's additional context
that we also consider.

00:17:15.303 --> 00:17:18.999
And that is what was happening in
your life outside of the classroom.

00:17:18.999 --> 00:17:22.713
So maybe you were on a varsity
sports team and we know the time

00:17:22.713 --> 00:17:26.814
commitment that is required if
you're a member of a sports team.

00:17:26.814 --> 00:17:29.220
Maybe you were working part time or
in some cases,

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:31.236
maybe full time while you were in school.

00:17:31.236 --> 00:17:34.756
And that might have impacted the amount
of time you were able to devote to your

00:17:34.756 --> 00:17:36.196
studies and your coursework.

00:17:36.196 --> 00:17:39.640
Maybe you were working on campus
serving as a teaching assistant or

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:40.876
a research assistant.

00:17:40.876 --> 00:17:44.452
So understanding the context
within which that you were

00:17:44.452 --> 00:17:49.103
pursuing your education is also really
important for us to understand.

00:17:49.103 --> 00:17:52.293
&gt;&gt; And I guess I want to mention too that,

00:17:52.293 --> 00:17:56.693
why is any of this important
in terms of your grades.

00:17:56.693 --> 00:18:00.957
I mean, as Christine mentioned, of course,
we want you not to just to survive here,

00:18:00.957 --> 00:18:01.857
but thrive here.

00:18:01.857 --> 00:18:05.986
But it's not like we want these grades

00:18:05.986 --> 00:18:10.899
because we are focused
on your achievement.

00:18:10.899 --> 00:18:14.468
A lot of the learning here at
Stanford is from each other.

00:18:14.468 --> 00:18:18.869
So we care a lot about if you're
the kind of person who loves ideas,

00:18:18.869 --> 00:18:23.666
is going to challenge each other and
the professor inside the classroom and

00:18:23.666 --> 00:18:25.332
outside the classroom.

00:18:25.332 --> 00:18:31.001
So a lot of that has to do not just
with want you to be a high achiever but

00:18:31.001 --> 00:18:36.971
we want you to be an active contributor
not only to your own education but

00:18:36.971 --> 00:18:38.786
to your classmates.

00:18:38.786 --> 00:18:43.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think on that front something
that I think of a lot about is if I

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:48.612
were In an applicant's shoes if I
was applying to business school,

00:18:48.612 --> 00:18:52.988
I know my academic history and
I could have prepared better.

00:18:52.988 --> 00:18:57.487
And so I think a lot about,
college was a different time in my life.

00:18:57.487 --> 00:19:02.871
So I actually think a lot
about is that who I am?

00:19:02.871 --> 00:19:07.433
Or are there things that we can do,
or that someone out there can do,

00:19:07.433 --> 00:19:12.316
to show that they have demonstrated or
that they have those abilities and

00:19:12.316 --> 00:19:16.424
can demonstrate it in a classroom or
learning environment.

00:19:16.424 --> 00:19:21.865
I think this might be the first time
we've ever actually gone into tips,

00:19:21.865 --> 00:19:23.208
best practices.

00:19:23.208 --> 00:19:26.305
Things folks out there
can do to go out and

00:19:26.305 --> 00:19:31.325
kind of take ownership of their
journey for lack of better words.

00:19:31.325 --> 00:19:35.991
And before we go into the space, I do want
to put out the disclaimer that we don't

00:19:35.991 --> 00:19:39.629
necessarily recommend that you
do one thing versus another.

00:19:39.629 --> 00:19:44.528
But really take that step back to see,
okay, what is realistic for for

00:19:44.528 --> 00:19:49.693
me given what whatever's around me
resources, time, accessibility.

00:19:49.693 --> 00:19:53.928
So again, this is not going to be
a set of hard and fast rules, but

00:19:53.928 --> 00:19:58.805
rather things that we might have seen and
maybe ways of thinking about it.

00:19:58.805 --> 00:20:05.036
That can help you even beyond
just the application process.

00:20:05.036 --> 00:20:07.399
We've had a lot of applications.

00:20:07.399 --> 00:20:11.965
What are some of the things that either
we've seen or kind of could think about

00:20:11.965 --> 00:20:15.844
for folks who want to beef up that
kind of academic preparedness?

00:20:18.978 --> 00:20:22.301
&gt;&gt; So in terms of that, well,
where I think you might be going here

00:20:22.301 --> 00:20:25.441
is if you're say coming from
social sciences background,

00:20:25.441 --> 00:20:27.860
you're an English major, a history major.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:31.829
Maybe you didn't really need to take
too many math or science classes and so

00:20:31.829 --> 00:20:35.432
you know we are going to be looking for
your quantitative exposure.

00:20:35.432 --> 00:20:40.249
You're not required to take pre-coursework
before you join the MBA, but

00:20:40.249 --> 00:20:42.320
some people do find it helpful.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:46.922
So you may want to take advantage of
all of those amazing online resources,

00:20:46.922 --> 00:20:48.685
local community colleges.

00:20:48.685 --> 00:20:52.035
There are a lot of ways to gain
exposure to some of those concepts.

00:20:52.035 --> 00:20:56.395
But it is entirely up to you, again, not
a requirement for our application process.

00:20:56.395 --> 00:20:59.008
You should check for
other schools that might have them.

00:20:59.008 --> 00:21:01.396
But for us, it's really about
just expanding your mind,

00:21:01.396 --> 00:21:03.503
getting comfortable with
some of these concepts.

00:21:03.503 --> 00:21:07.958
And some of those do come with fees, but
many of those can actually be accessed for

00:21:07.958 --> 00:21:08.951
free online now.

00:21:08.951 --> 00:21:11.269
So there is a way to gain that exposure.

00:21:11.269 --> 00:21:14.058
And we have seen some people
do it before they apply.

00:21:14.058 --> 00:21:17.792
Others don't do it before they apply, but
they make the choice to actually gain some

00:21:17.792 --> 00:21:20.069
of that experience before
they start the program.

00:21:20.069 --> 00:21:23.658
So in the summer leading up to school,
sometimes they'll take advantage of some

00:21:23.658 --> 00:21:26.791
of those online resources or
taking a class before they start the MBA.

00:21:26.791 --> 00:21:30.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so Christine,
what happens if I'm signed up for

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:35.580
a class that is after the decision or
after the application deadline?

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:36.552
How should I convey that?

00:21:36.552 --> 00:21:37.406
Should I convey it?

00:21:37.406 --> 00:21:38.962
Is that important?

00:21:38.962 --> 00:21:40.999
&gt;&gt; Absolutely,
it's certainly worth mentioning.

00:21:40.999 --> 00:21:41.946
So when in doubt,

00:21:41.946 --> 00:21:46.244
whenever you feel like there might be some
piece of your intellectual or academic

00:21:46.244 --> 00:21:50.746
journey or story that can't be clearly
explained or expressed in the application.

00:21:50.746 --> 00:21:55.241
That is where the additional information
section will come in in your application,

00:21:55.241 --> 00:21:59.102
which is not meant to be just a whole
separate essay, but rather a place as

00:21:59.102 --> 00:22:02.929
a catch all really, for anything that
you just need to inform us about.

00:22:02.929 --> 00:22:07.117
So it could be a single bullet point or
sentence that says I've registered to take

00:22:07.117 --> 00:22:11.446
this class and I'll be starting it and it
could be after the application deadline.

00:22:11.446 --> 00:22:16.452
This is also a place where if you had any
struggles or challenges during undergrad,

00:22:16.452 --> 00:22:19.826
either an overall GPA or
maybe just a particular class.

00:22:19.826 --> 00:22:22.943
If you feel like there's additional
context that you need to explain to us,

00:22:22.943 --> 00:22:24.795
for where those grades
might have come from,

00:22:24.795 --> 00:22:27.146
you can use that additional
information section there.

00:22:27.146 --> 00:22:30.679
We've definitely seen that utilized for
anyone who was explaining whether it

00:22:30.679 --> 00:22:34.068
was an illness or an injury that may
have prevented them from taking a test.

00:22:34.068 --> 00:22:36.964
And that affected their final grade in
a class, that you can let us know there.

00:22:36.964 --> 00:22:40.608
There is a space so you don't leave us
to our own devices to make up stories.

00:22:40.608 --> 00:22:43.766
You can tell us exactly what happened.

00:22:43.766 --> 00:22:48.311
&gt;&gt; And I would encourage folks to think
about if you're going to do any of

00:22:48.311 --> 00:22:50.352
this academic preparation.

00:22:50.352 --> 00:22:55.096
Maybe it's just been a long time
since you've had to do either

00:22:55.096 --> 00:22:57.568
intensive reading or writing.

00:22:57.568 --> 00:23:02.574
To think about preparing for the MBA
itself, you know, work towards a goal

00:23:02.574 --> 00:23:07.276
that you'll be able to hit the ground
running once you're at school.

00:23:07.276 --> 00:23:11.701
because that is going to help you long
term as opposed to just taking a class for

00:23:11.701 --> 00:23:13.135
the sake of applying.

00:23:13.135 --> 00:23:19.166
And I hope that mentality will set
you up better for long term success.

00:23:19.166 --> 00:23:21.760
In terms of what classes to think about,

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:25.510
you actually know your kind
of academic history the best.

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:30.554
And this is where I would encourage you
to take that kind of step back again,

00:23:30.554 --> 00:23:32.969
and take control of the narrative.

00:23:32.969 --> 00:23:37.093
If you know there's an area that you
might have struggled with in the past,

00:23:37.093 --> 00:23:41.294
that could be an area to think about
doing a little bit of additional work on.

00:23:41.294 --> 00:23:44.504
We talk a lot about
quantitative skills and

00:23:44.504 --> 00:23:49.328
the quantitative elements of
the business school experience.

00:23:49.328 --> 00:23:54.345
But I would also encourage folks to
Practice reading practice writing,

00:23:54.345 --> 00:23:59.500
communication and verbal skills are also
going to be a huge part of MBA experience.

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:03.504
I know Allison was just talking
about how do you challenge ideas and

00:24:03.504 --> 00:24:08.083
have constructive dialogue where there
are differences of opinion because

00:24:08.083 --> 00:24:10.960
that's where learning happens.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:16.491
I think Dean Levin talks about
learning happens when you embrace

00:24:16.491 --> 00:24:21.650
the difference and so
how are we able to communicate that?

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:26.770
So we covered a little bit about
transcripts so now can we let's

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:31.792
just shift over to testing like
there's a lot of questions and

00:24:31.792 --> 00:24:35.944
even in our q&amp;a box about,
okay, the test score,

00:24:35.944 --> 00:24:41.010
you get one score, but
there's also sub components.

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:43.295
Can we talk a little bit about that and

00:24:43.295 --> 00:24:47.590
how that kind of factors in among
the other parts of the application.

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:51.938
&gt;&gt; I guess I'd start by just reminding
people that just like everything in

00:24:51.938 --> 00:24:53.097
the application,

00:24:53.097 --> 00:24:58.690
we keep telling you that we look at it in
context same thing with the test scores.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:04.640
So for example, the verbal score we're not
going to expect as high a verbal score,

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:08.636
for a non-native speaker as
a native English speaker,

00:25:08.636 --> 00:25:12.240
of course with a quantitative score.

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:17.221
If you were that History or
English major, you may not, and

00:25:17.221 --> 00:25:22.499
you probably won't have as high
a quant score as that physics or

00:25:22.499 --> 00:25:27.679
engineering majors so,
we look at each piece in context and,

00:25:27.679 --> 00:25:31.550
as will said, there's no minimum score.

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:34.521
But I should say with
the English proficiency exams,

00:25:34.521 --> 00:25:38.811
there are hard minimums, for obvious
reasons you need to be fluent in English

00:25:38.811 --> 00:25:41.997
before you get here but
with the GMAT, GRE, no minimum.

00:25:41.997 --> 00:25:46.197
&gt;&gt; And remind me, Alison, those are
actually set by the university as a whole,

00:25:46.197 --> 00:25:46.788
correct?

00:25:46.788 --> 00:25:48.432
&gt;&gt; For the English proficiency.

00:25:48.432 --> 00:25:49.183
Right?
Yeah.

00:25:49.183 --> 00:25:52.004
&gt;&gt; But well,
since we're talking about tests,

00:25:52.004 --> 00:25:56.777
I'd love to remind everyone now that
whether we're speaking about the GMAT,

00:25:56.777 --> 00:26:01.840
GRE or the English language proficiency
exams, you all have options.

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:03.568
So we accept both the GMAT and

00:26:03.568 --> 00:26:07.670
the GRE and there are no preferences
between those two exams.

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:11.912
So usually what I personally recommend and
speaking for myself, but before you sign

00:26:11.912 --> 00:26:16.035
to take another test you can take free
practice questions online with both tests,

00:26:16.035 --> 00:26:18.899
so you can get a sense or a feel for
what each test is like and

00:26:18.899 --> 00:26:22.006
if you find that you might be
scoring better versus the other,

00:26:22.006 --> 00:26:25.810
maybe that's the test to consider and
that might be the one you want to take.

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:29.228
If you think that'll give you the
opportunity to put your best foot forward

00:26:29.228 --> 00:26:30.855
there on a testing standpoint, and

00:26:30.855 --> 00:26:33.946
when it comes to the English language
proficiency is we accept three

00:26:33.946 --> 00:26:37.680
different tests that are TOEFL the IELTS
and the Pearson test of English.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:41.241
So yes, there are minimums for each of
those that are set by Stanford University

00:26:41.241 --> 00:26:44.855
but again, there are different tests and
ways of asking you those questions so,

00:26:44.855 --> 00:26:48.020
you can be selective in terms of
which tests you're choosing to take.

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:53.147
&gt;&gt; And can we have a very wide
range of scores in the class,

00:26:53.147 --> 00:26:56.888
you will see,
high average scores at Stanford and

00:26:56.888 --> 00:27:01.880
it's not because we're in aiming for
particular score that those

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:07.880
averages tend to reflect our applicant
pool in terms of who applies.

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:13.497
But many times we've had people in
the class, into the five hundreds and

00:27:13.497 --> 00:27:18.655
I can remember one particular case
where we actually had someone in

00:27:18.655 --> 00:27:24.456
the class who got 450 GMAT now not
saying you should aim for 450 GMAT,

00:27:24.456 --> 00:27:30.469
but the point was the person had quite
a low score and they did perfectly fine.

00:27:30.469 --> 00:27:35.563
&gt;&gt; And so I think that also goes into our
conversation about the sub scores one,

00:27:35.563 --> 00:27:40.737
of them, again, is not necessarily more or
less important than the other and

00:27:40.737 --> 00:27:44.877
then we're looking across both
your academic history as well

00:27:44.877 --> 00:27:49.540
as what are some of the things
you're doing in your workplace.

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:54.080
I think that is somewhere that folks don't
often think about when we're thinking

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:58.027
about that intellectual vitality and
what are some of the skill sets and

00:27:58.027 --> 00:28:02.940
knowledge sets that are going to help set
you up for success in business school?

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:08.014
And that's actually one of the questions
here from user who's asking

00:28:08.014 --> 00:28:13.002
about those subsections and
I can say that I have seen instances where

00:28:13.002 --> 00:28:19.200
someone did not have that many math
courses in their academic background.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:21.948
Testing was fine and in their workplace,

00:28:21.948 --> 00:28:25.718
they were doing a lot of
financial analysis modeling so,

00:28:25.718 --> 00:28:30.588
it demonstrated ok, yes this individual
does have that wherewithal to be

00:28:30.588 --> 00:28:36.140
able to thrive in different class
settings and in different environments.

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:41.021
At Business School, so thinking about
it even holistically and very broadly

00:28:41.021 --> 00:28:45.850
beyond just here's that one section and
what does that one thing tell us?

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:48.489
Again, it's like no one
pieces like entry gate, but

00:28:48.489 --> 00:28:52.930
really we're looking at the entirety
of your application to find things.

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:57.356
&gt;&gt; That's something to that
your recommender might mention,

00:28:57.356 --> 00:29:01.609
they might talk about Analytical
projects you've done or

00:29:01.609 --> 00:29:05.601
projects that required a lot
of reading and writing and

00:29:05.601 --> 00:29:10.829
give us another data point about
your quantitative and verbal skills.

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:16.014
&gt;&gt; Okay?
And because I mentioned the word holistic,

00:29:16.014 --> 00:29:20.687
maybe a million times during this
conversation, there's actually

00:29:20.687 --> 00:29:25.357
a question here from Alex,
about how much time does does an admission

00:29:25.357 --> 00:29:30.380
officers spend to holistically
evaluate a candidate any thoughts?

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:34.440
I have a thought but I'm really curious
to hear what your responses are.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:38.947
&gt;&gt; Well you notice that Christina and
I both have rather thick laughs.

00:29:38.947 --> 00:29:39.485
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:29:39.485 --> 00:29:43.075
&gt;&gt; And that's no accident over so
many years of reading.

00:29:43.075 --> 00:29:44.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:29:44.420 --> 00:29:45.155
&gt;&gt; Absolutely,

00:29:45.155 --> 00:29:48.335
and I would say I you probably saw
me chuckle there when you were

00:29:48.335 --> 00:29:53.580
asking the question because I think we all
read at our own different personal pieces.

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:57.655
I know how much time it takes me to read
my change depending on what time of day it

00:29:57.655 --> 00:29:59.536
is so, the most important thing for

00:29:59.536 --> 00:30:02.798
us is that we are reviewing every
piece of that application and

00:30:02.798 --> 00:30:07.480
some people might read a little faster,
some might read a little slower.

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:11.387
Some people might read and then take
longer to really think about, making any

00:30:11.387 --> 00:30:15.533
decisions and so we're all kind of working
at our own paces, but we're all on that

00:30:15.533 --> 00:30:19.560
same mission to make sure we're treating
every applicant with full respect and

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:24.160
reading every portion of that application
before reaching any conclusions.

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:29.013
So the actual number would probably vary
pretty widely depending on the day or

00:30:29.013 --> 00:30:33.150
the time of year even
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I will, go ahead Allison.

00:30:33.150 --> 00:30:38.039
&gt;&gt; I was just going to say for us, it's
sort of like peeling back the onion so,

00:30:38.039 --> 00:30:43.750
for example, if we do see a low GPA, we're
going to be asking ourselves, why is that?

00:30:43.750 --> 00:30:45.580
Is there any circumstances there?

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:50.890
Is there any countervailing
evidence like GMAT score

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:56.610
GRE score to mitigate the GPA or
performance at work?

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:57.820
So.

00:30:57.820 --> 00:31:02.793
&gt;&gt; And my half joking half serious answer
is as long as it takes some applications

00:31:02.793 --> 00:31:07.153
are pretty clear cut like,
here's someone evidence is here,

00:31:07.153 --> 00:31:10.640
it's clear there's
shining recommendations.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:12.430
Okay?
Here's someone we might want to

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:16.691
consider it those ones go quickly, they're
also somewhat, maybe the preparation

00:31:16.691 --> 00:31:20.773
is here we're not seeing some of that
demonstrated leadership potential so.,

00:31:20.773 --> 00:31:22.930
those might go quickly as well.

00:31:22.930 --> 00:31:27.758
And then the ones that take the longest
are often the ones that have the pieces

00:31:27.758 --> 00:31:32.815
contextual information or dots that we
need to do a little bit more connecting,

00:31:32.815 --> 00:31:37.644
so that like, as Christine was saying,
like we are giving a fair and like how

00:31:37.644 --> 00:31:43.020
can we best honor the entirety of this
application to give a fair assessment?

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:44.451
And so it really does.

00:31:44.451 --> 00:31:50.590
Differ by individual kind of environmental
conditions and also by application.

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:53.436
So a couple of different factors there.

00:31:53.436 --> 00:31:58.961
But at the end, I actually really value
and appreciate our reading system.

00:31:58.961 --> 00:32:03.285
And it's actually one of the things that
keeps me in this job is that we're able to

00:32:03.285 --> 00:32:04.694
do things holistically.

00:32:04.694 --> 00:32:07.013
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:32:07.013 --> 00:32:08.624
&gt;&gt; So let's go into,

00:32:08.624 --> 00:32:13.880
we're actually going to start
leaning more into the Q and A box.

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:17.656
I think there's some really
great content here, Christine,

00:32:17.656 --> 00:32:22.660
there was a question that I saw
earlier about deferred candidates.

00:32:22.660 --> 00:32:27.510
Like they still have a chunk of their
senior year usually left to go.

00:32:27.510 --> 00:32:32.569
So, for our different candidate and for
admission, that is when you're applying in

00:32:32.569 --> 00:32:37.359
the final year of your bachelors or a grad
program if you gone all the way through.

00:32:37.359 --> 00:32:40.104
Can you talk a little bit of
how we view those transcripts,

00:32:40.104 --> 00:32:43.747
knowing that there's going to be a little
bit a chunk of them that's not going to be

00:32:43.747 --> 00:32:45.222
full, by the time they apply?

00:32:45.222 --> 00:32:48.640
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, well and again, we're
going to use that word context again.

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:53.210
It's about us understanding what you've
done with the time you've had so far.

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:55.510
So, for
students that have been working for

00:32:55.510 --> 00:32:59.624
five years will have the full kind of past
tense view of how you did an undergrad.

00:32:59.624 --> 00:33:02.516
With our deferred students who might
be applying during their senior year,

00:33:02.516 --> 00:33:05.750
we'll see how you've done in your first
three, or three and a half years.

00:33:05.750 --> 00:33:10.281
So we'll get a sense of how you performed
in the classroom most recently,

00:33:10.281 --> 00:33:15.178
you'll be able to take the tests while
you're still in that kind of study mode,

00:33:15.178 --> 00:33:17.280
while you're still a student.

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:20.484
And so, that will give us just
kind of a moment in time,

00:33:20.484 --> 00:33:23.703
understanding of how you
performed in the classroom.

00:33:23.703 --> 00:33:26.596
So our expectation that is if
that you're performing well now,

00:33:26.596 --> 00:33:29.233
hopefully you are preparing and
will be performing well.

00:33:29.233 --> 00:33:32.647
Two to four years from now and you might
be joining us in the MBA classroom,

00:33:32.647 --> 00:33:34.810
but it is just a piece and
it is a moment in time.

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:38.721
So, like with all of our applications,
these are just some of the data that we're

00:33:38.721 --> 00:33:41.393
considering when reviewing
your entire application.

00:33:41.393 --> 00:33:47.041
&gt;&gt; And we will ask for that final
transcript for that last semester,

00:33:47.041 --> 00:33:52.286
[LAUGH] in the summer before you come so,
keep your grades up.

00:33:52.286 --> 00:33:56.474
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Here's a tactical practical question.

00:33:56.474 --> 00:34:02.324
So going into some of the logistics of
it and this question is from Konica.

00:34:02.324 --> 00:34:06.346
Are we open to considering the highest
section scores from different sittings?

00:34:06.346 --> 00:34:09.932
Or do we consider the overall
like a single sitting or

00:34:09.932 --> 00:34:12.385
the overall highest G-Maggiore?

00:34:12.385 --> 00:34:18.337
&gt;&gt; Good question, but officially we
will need you to report a single score.

00:34:18.337 --> 00:34:22.280
So from a single sitting, so you may
have taken the test say two times and

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:25.129
you had a higher verbal on one and
a higher on other.

00:34:25.129 --> 00:34:28.170
You can't mix them together and
you'll have to pick one of

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:31.400
those overall scores to report
to us in your application, and

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:33.900
you'll be self reporting
in the application.

00:34:33.900 --> 00:34:36.540
I think I saw another question in
that Q and A box that, what if I take

00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:39.880
the test and the official scores may
not be in, in time for the deadline?

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:42.400
That's okay, because you're self
reporting on the application.

00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:46.070
It's okay if the official scores post
right after were a little after that

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:47.320
application deadline.

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:48.329
But ultimately,

00:34:48.329 --> 00:34:52.977
you can only there's only space in the
application to report one full test score.

00:34:54.381 --> 00:34:58.018
&gt;&gt; Here's a question from Catarina and
I have to admit I love this question.

00:34:58.018 --> 00:35:02.354
I love when we're able to unpack,
and demystify, and

00:35:02.354 --> 00:35:07.172
really add information and
me too rumors that are out there.

00:35:07.172 --> 00:35:12.103
And so here's one who they've heard from
previous students that the bar is much

00:35:12.103 --> 00:35:14.460
higher for international students,

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:18.523
are the expectations higher because
we come from outside the US?

00:35:18.523 --> 00:35:21.120
Let's discuss this one.

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:22.570
Let's dig into this one.

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:25.429
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
we love busting these myths in it,

00:35:25.429 --> 00:35:30.640
it makes our blood pressure go up,
we hit some of these nerves.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:35.638
I would say that the only difference
is that English proficiency exam that

00:35:35.638 --> 00:35:39.360
you do need to be fluent in
English before you get here.

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:43.857
And so yes, the,
I wouldn't say the bar is higher, but

00:35:43.857 --> 00:35:49.795
there is a far minimum on each of those
three English proficiency tests and

00:35:49.795 --> 00:35:52.502
you do need to meet that minimum.

00:35:52.502 --> 00:35:55.811
And I don't know Christine,
that's the only difference I can think of.

00:35:55.811 --> 00:36:00.840
&gt;&gt; I would agree because, otherwise,
when we're looking at the G-Maggiore,

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:05.639
or the transcripts, we're looking at
everything exactly as we as we do for

00:36:05.639 --> 00:36:07.793
every application that we read.

00:36:07.793 --> 00:36:12.715
So, looking for those same instances or
examples of intellectual vitality and

00:36:12.715 --> 00:36:13.940
curiosity.

00:36:13.940 --> 00:36:17.923
And the context in which we consider how
people ended up with the grades that they

00:36:17.923 --> 00:36:21.370
did, through all of the things that
Allison had mentioned earlier,

00:36:21.370 --> 00:36:24.818
the majors and other things that
might have been happening outside,

00:36:24.818 --> 00:36:29.410
all of that we look at every application
in with the same kind of perspective.

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:31.501
So it really is just those
minimums that again,

00:36:31.501 --> 00:36:34.274
are set by Stanford, and
the reason those minimums are there,

00:36:34.274 --> 00:36:37.720
is because the the coursework here
is taught entirely in English.

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:41.565
Again, we do want to make sure that people
will not only be just kind of getting by

00:36:41.565 --> 00:36:45.430
in the classroom but being active
participants in the experience.

00:36:45.430 --> 00:36:51.390
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I would also say that we
read a lot of international applications.

00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:54.855
So we also have a very
good sense of what some of

00:36:54.855 --> 00:36:58.330
the different academic
kind of systems are.

00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:02.710
And also,
not everybody is graded on the same scale.

00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:06.972
And so sometimes when people are trying
to translate or do correlations,

00:37:06.972 --> 00:37:10.770
I think there's a lot that gets
lost in translation there.

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:15.849
I know there was another
question of crier in the Q and

00:37:15.849 --> 00:37:20.249
A box that had to do like
what GPA do we report?

00:37:20.249 --> 00:37:20.936
We do, there it is.

00:37:20.936 --> 00:37:21.874
Do we use it for

00:37:21.874 --> 00:37:26.272
each unique year or do we use
the cumulative at the end of each year?

00:37:26.272 --> 00:37:30.192
So, not all schools will do
that report what you have, and

00:37:30.192 --> 00:37:35.770
I would say reported in the grading system
and grading scale that you attended.

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:39.732
We want to see how you did in
the context of your education.

00:37:39.732 --> 00:37:42.994
And even within the American system,
I could see there's like,

00:37:42.994 --> 00:37:44.370
I've seen 4.0 scales.

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:45.922
I've seen 5.0 scales.

00:37:45.922 --> 00:37:50.238
I've seen there are schools that
don't give us a grade, a letter or

00:37:50.238 --> 00:37:54.720
a number but there's a full write up for
every course they've taken.

00:37:55.780 --> 00:37:57.883
Those are fun to work through.

00:37:57.883 --> 00:38:03.130
So we are very [LAUGH] comfortable
with a wide range of these systems.

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:04.559
But, as both Alison and

00:38:04.559 --> 00:38:08.992
Christine were saying like there is no
different standard that we hold for

00:38:08.992 --> 00:38:13.938
international candidates, I think that
context piece does play come into play.

00:38:13.938 --> 00:38:18.382
Sometimes the language element because
maybe your first, second, and or

00:38:18.382 --> 00:38:23.036
maybe third or beyond languages were not
English, and that might pull down and

00:38:23.036 --> 00:38:26.090
overall geomat score make
things look different.

00:38:26.090 --> 00:38:29.600
So, I would set that myth to rest.

00:38:29.600 --> 00:38:34.562
We are looking for the same criteria, and
the same types of talent, ability and

00:38:34.562 --> 00:38:39.529
perspective or a breadth of talent,
skills and perspectives diversity within

00:38:39.529 --> 00:38:44.141
diversity from [LAUGH] all over Africans,
no matter where they come from.

00:38:44.141 --> 00:38:49.480
Alright, question, continuing on
this theme of international and

00:38:49.480 --> 00:38:55.095
academics and other contexts,
if our transcripts aren't in English,

00:38:55.095 --> 00:38:58.100
do we need to have them translated?

00:38:58.100 --> 00:38:59.621
Is it okay to translate them ourselves?

00:38:59.621 --> 00:39:01.489
How should someone approach that?

00:39:05.095 --> 00:39:10.361
&gt;&gt; Yes, when you send the copies of the
transcripts, often times we may see both,

00:39:10.361 --> 00:39:14.134
so, we may see the official
in your original language and

00:39:14.134 --> 00:39:16.570
then a translated version.

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:21.146
Because despite our best efforts,
we can't read all of the languages, but

00:39:21.146 --> 00:39:23.378
it is sometimes helpful to see both.

00:39:23.378 --> 00:39:24.588
But, some of you,

00:39:24.588 --> 00:39:29.149
sometimes people will use different
services to do the translations.

00:39:29.149 --> 00:39:32.520
So, there's some flexibility when you're
applying, because we're looking for

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:35.050
copies of your transcripts at this time.

00:39:35.050 --> 00:39:38.422
But if admitted, we will need to
see the official transcripts and

00:39:38.422 --> 00:39:41.260
we will need an official
translated copy at that time.

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:45.734
Thank you.

00:39:45.734 --> 00:39:49.997
And so you just mentioned,
can apply with an unofficial transcript,

00:39:49.997 --> 00:39:52.249
will request an official one later.

00:39:52.249 --> 00:39:54.999
I know sometimes that has
been a topic of conversation,

00:39:54.999 --> 00:39:58.923
we understand that sometimes requesting
transcripts cost additional money,

00:39:58.923 --> 00:40:03.140
if you're applying to many business
schools, that might increase the cost.

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:07.630
So we're really trying to be as sensitive
as possible throughout this process.

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:11.473
Also knowing that sometimes it's just
really difficult to get official

00:40:11.473 --> 00:40:16.650
transcripts, especially if maybe you
haven't attended school for a long time.

00:40:16.650 --> 00:40:19.150
There can be a lot of different
hoops you have to jump through.

00:40:19.150 --> 00:40:22.770
So we're trying our best to ease
that process wherever we can.

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:27.298
Okay, and here's another question and

00:40:27.298 --> 00:40:30.273
I think it tailors nicely
with this question of,

00:40:30.273 --> 00:40:35.730
maybe I'm still doing some preparation,
maybe there's things that I'm still doing.

00:40:36.920 --> 00:40:40.670
When should someone apply, right?

00:40:40.670 --> 00:40:45.370
What point should we be thinking about, I
need to have everything in my application,

00:40:45.370 --> 00:40:48.326
or what are things that it's
okay to update later on?

00:40:48.326 --> 00:40:49.680
When does that make sense?

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:54.280
How should I be thinking about kind
of the two possibilities out there?

00:40:55.750 --> 00:41:00.253
&gt;&gt; And then of course, obviously
there's when you want to start school,

00:41:00.253 --> 00:41:02.619
which is probably the first cut, but

00:41:02.619 --> 00:41:06.994
then it's really when can you
present the strongest application.

00:41:06.994 --> 00:41:10.464
And when you update things
later after the deadline,

00:41:10.464 --> 00:41:16.014
they will become part of your file but we
can't promise that they'll be considered,

00:41:16.014 --> 00:41:19.310
we may have already read your application.

00:41:19.310 --> 00:41:23.722
So I would say, if you're waiting for
something really important,

00:41:23.722 --> 00:41:27.903
like well, in fact, with the GMAT,
if you're going to retake it or

00:41:27.903 --> 00:41:31.950
the GRE score, that does need
to be taken before the deadline.

00:41:31.950 --> 00:41:35.383
So, I would say,
minor things could be updated, but

00:41:35.383 --> 00:41:40.193
if it's something really important
to the strength of your application,

00:41:40.193 --> 00:41:42.580
you'd want to do it before you apply.

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:43.886
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:41:43.886 --> 00:41:47.189
And here, let's round into this
question again, kind of we've

00:41:47.189 --> 00:41:52.450
approached it from different perspectives,
but I don't think we've actually named it.

00:41:52.450 --> 00:41:56.015
So the question is,
from someone who does not necessarily

00:41:56.015 --> 00:42:00.890
have the broadest quant background, and
maybe some of the obvious places, but

00:42:00.890 --> 00:42:04.520
how can they communicate
quantitative abilities?

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:09.041
Do they always need to, or
in what spaces does it make sense?

00:42:09.041 --> 00:42:14.071
&gt;&gt; Yep, I think Alison talked about this
a little bit earlier on in that again,

00:42:14.071 --> 00:42:18.866
if it doesn't naturally or obviously
present in, say your test score or

00:42:18.866 --> 00:42:21.952
in your academic background and
experience.

00:42:21.952 --> 00:42:24.722
The workplace is oftentimes
where this will surface.

00:42:24.722 --> 00:42:28.980
And in some cases, it might present
because you've told us in your resume,

00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:32.639
maybe one of the line items under
one of your particular roles, or

00:42:32.639 --> 00:42:34.635
your time at a particular company,

00:42:34.635 --> 00:42:39.179
you're able to highlight a time when you
were working on something analytical.

00:42:39.179 --> 00:42:41.406
But another place where
that surfaces a lot for

00:42:41.406 --> 00:42:44.100
us is through the letters of
reference that we receive.

00:42:44.100 --> 00:42:48.531
So your recommenders may be able to share
stories with us about how you were able to

00:42:48.531 --> 00:42:52.116
display some of those quantitative or
analytical skills through

00:42:52.116 --> 00:42:56.044
a different project that you might
have been working on in the company.

00:42:56.044 --> 00:42:58.472
So there's a lot of different places,
and again,

00:42:58.472 --> 00:43:01.079
even sometimes in your
extracurricular activities.

00:43:01.079 --> 00:43:05.703
So, you may not be giving yourself enough
credit, you may be a part of a community

00:43:05.703 --> 00:43:08.560
organization and
you had to organize a big drive.

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:11.709
And it turns out that you actually had to
keep things really organized and you were

00:43:11.709 --> 00:43:14.737
actually using more of those kind of
muscles than you may have even realized.

00:43:14.737 --> 00:43:17.600
So that may be an opportunity to share
some of those stories within your

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:19.350
application as well.

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:22.610
&gt;&gt; Yeah, maybe you're sharing some of
that in one of your written responses.

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:25.722
Maybe it's an optional response,
maybe just a bullet point or

00:43:25.722 --> 00:43:30.370
two in your additional information if it
doesn't come up naturally somewhere else.

00:43:30.370 --> 00:43:33.033
So we really provide
a lot of landscape for

00:43:33.033 --> 00:43:35.781
you to use in a way that makes sense for
you.

00:43:35.781 --> 00:43:40.581
So hopefully, if you're thinking about it,
and you're like, yes,

00:43:40.581 --> 00:43:45.222
I do have these but it doesn't come
out obviously on my application,

00:43:45.222 --> 00:43:47.955
let us know in one of these other spaces.

00:43:47.955 --> 00:43:50.702
One more question that I
really want to ask our group.

00:43:50.702 --> 00:43:55.775
I have come across, I've had a lot
of conversations about folks who

00:43:55.775 --> 00:44:01.471
might not have had the most stellar
kind of initial undergraduate career and

00:44:01.471 --> 00:44:05.298
then re-did or
had these other kind of journeys and

00:44:05.298 --> 00:44:09.050
pathways that show that
they have these talents.

00:44:09.050 --> 00:44:16.309
So, as we close out this session, kind of,
can we crystallize in just a few words,

00:44:16.309 --> 00:44:22.877
how can you demonstrate this intellectual
vitality across a broad range?

00:44:22.877 --> 00:44:28.267
Any thoughts, words of wisdom, words of
encouragement that you have for folks

00:44:28.267 --> 00:44:33.346
out there, who might still be hesitating
because of a past that they have?

00:44:35.412 --> 00:44:39.974
&gt;&gt; I guess mine are just a few reminders
that you do have that additional

00:44:39.974 --> 00:44:45.350
information section if you do want to
explain some extenuating circumstances.

00:44:45.350 --> 00:44:49.227
And also that we look
at the whole journey so

00:44:49.227 --> 00:44:56.012
you may have started out with a weaker
GPA in the beginning of university and

00:44:56.012 --> 00:45:00.212
then it got stronger, or
you may have done some

00:45:00.212 --> 00:45:05.800
graduate work that had
showed stronger performance.

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:10.311
And that, again, we look at other
sources like your workplace,

00:45:10.311 --> 00:45:14.578
your extracurriculars,
maybe you're in a robotics club and

00:45:14.578 --> 00:45:18.531
my goodness,
you've done a lot of quantitative stuff.

00:45:18.531 --> 00:45:21.666
&gt;&gt; I'll just kind of add on
top of what Alison mentioned,

00:45:21.666 --> 00:45:26.934
remembering that your academic experience,
undergrad or graduate, if you've had any.

00:45:26.934 --> 00:45:31.252
Again, it's a moment in time in
your very long life and journey.

00:45:31.252 --> 00:45:34.054
And so if you didn't perform
as well in undergrad for

00:45:34.054 --> 00:45:36.231
whatever reasons that might have been.

00:45:36.231 --> 00:45:39.677
Knowing that you'll still have
an opportunity to demonstrate some of that

00:45:39.677 --> 00:45:43.670
intellectual vitality through the testing
process, that could be an opportunity for

00:45:43.670 --> 00:45:46.971
you to make sure you're spending
enough time studying and preparing.

00:45:46.971 --> 00:45:51.315
So you have an opportunity to show what
we may not have been able to see earlier.

00:45:51.315 --> 00:45:55.275
If you're one of those folks out there who
worry about testing that's not your strong

00:45:55.275 --> 00:45:57.540
suit, I understand, I hear you.

00:45:57.540 --> 00:46:01.610
And just know that if you are coming in
maybe with a lower GMAT or GRE in lower

00:46:01.610 --> 00:46:06.271
grades, again that additional information
up there is a section where you can share

00:46:06.271 --> 00:46:10.301
some of that contacts the rest of
the story to fill in those pieces for us.

00:46:10.301 --> 00:46:14.612
And gain, just know that we are looking at
everything else that you've done in your

00:46:14.612 --> 00:46:18.741
life beyond those two things, to try to
get full understanding of who you are and

00:46:18.741 --> 00:46:21.227
what you have to contribute
to this community.

00:46:21.227 --> 00:46:25.513
&gt;&gt; And I love how we've kind of zoomed
out through this process to really think

00:46:25.513 --> 00:46:26.670
high level.

00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:30.135
My words of encouragement are you are so

00:46:30.135 --> 00:46:34.690
much more than two numbers
in an application.

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:39.390
There is the journey, as part of
that journey is that learning and

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:42.740
growth that you have
earned over your time.

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:45.579
If you're thinking about business school,

00:46:45.579 --> 00:46:48.740
who are you now because
of those experiences?

00:46:48.740 --> 00:46:51.862
What are some of the skill
sets that you now have so

00:46:51.862 --> 00:46:55.909
that you can thrive when you are in
a moment of challenge again?

00:46:55.909 --> 00:47:00.806
What are some of the things that do
spark your excitement to learn, grow and

00:47:00.806 --> 00:47:03.771
in turn, share that knowledge with others?

00:47:03.771 --> 00:47:08.706
I think, as we kind of step back and
really think about the wholeness

00:47:08.706 --> 00:47:13.880
of who you are, the academic piece
is just one part of the story.

00:47:13.880 --> 00:47:17.464
And I really encourage you,
if you aren't already,

00:47:17.464 --> 00:47:22.878
check out our Sharing Your Story Workshop
Series, where we'll be able to impact

00:47:22.878 --> 00:47:28.660
even more about how you can think about
these elements and moments in your life.

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:31.685
Such that you can really
put your best foot forward,

00:47:31.685 --> 00:47:36.510
whether it's in a written format in
the application and interview, networking.

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:39.493
Or any other place where people
are trying to learn more about you and

00:47:39.493 --> 00:47:43.920
you're building relationships, kind of
to move your career and life forward.

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:48.122
With that, if you haven't done so,
I also encourage you,

00:47:48.122 --> 00:47:51.736
I'm doing a lot of
encouraging today apparently,

00:47:51.736 --> 00:47:56.034
[LAUGH] I encourage you to start
at MyStanford.mba account.

00:47:56.034 --> 00:47:59.924
With a couple questions we can
help you create a dashboard

00:47:59.924 --> 00:48:03.338
that will consolidate
events of interest to you.

00:48:03.338 --> 00:48:07.004
There's also an application tracker
that'll help you keep track

00:48:07.004 --> 00:48:11.191
of where you are in the process to make
sure you're headed in the right way for

00:48:11.191 --> 00:48:14.214
that round one, round two,
or round three deadline.

00:48:14.214 --> 00:48:18.342
With that, thank you so
much for joining us today.

00:48:18.342 --> 00:48:22.947
Hopefully, we were able to share some
good information, things that help you

00:48:22.947 --> 00:48:27.416
better understand how we use Testing
Transcripts and your overall academic

00:48:27.416 --> 00:48:31.630
history to better understand who you
are in this application process.

00:48:33.070 --> 00:48:35.365
Hopefully, I'll see you around
throughout the rest of this week or

00:48:35.365 --> 00:48:36.760
at other Stanford events.

00:48:36.760 --> 00:48:39.267
Until then, take care and stay safe.

